sections.body:
The net/http package is built around a tiny interface:

```go
type Handler interface {
	ServeHTTP(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)
}
```

That's the whole definition: one method, no return value. It follows the same small-interface idea as `io.Reader` in the Packages and Standard Library concept, whose single `Read` method lets unrelated types work with `io.Copy`. Anything with a matching `ServeHTTP` method satisfies `http.Handler` implicitly, with no `implements` keyword:

```go
type greeter struct {
	name string
}

func (g greeter) ServeHTTP(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	fmt.Fprintf(w, "Hello, %s!", g.name)
}
```

`greeter` never mentions `http.Handler`, yet a `greeter` value can be handed to anything in net/http that expects one, because it has the right method. `ServeHTTP` is responsible for handling one request from start to finish: it reads what it needs from `r *http.Request` (method, URL, headers, body) and writes the response through `w http.ResponseWriter`. There is no return value for the response; everything the client receives is a side effect of what `ServeHTTP` does to `w` before it returns.

`http.ResponseWriter` has three methods to know: `Header()` returns the response's header map, which you can freely modify **before** anything else happens; `WriteHeader(statusCode int)` sends the status line and headers to the client; and `Write(data []byte)` sends body bytes, implicitly calling `WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)` first **if `WriteHeader` hasn't already been called explicitly**.

This has a sharp consequence: **once the status code has been sent, either by an explicit `WriteHeader` call or implicitly by the first `Write`, it cannot be changed.** A common mistake is writing some body content first, then deciding an error occurred and trying to send a different status code:

```go
func buggyHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	fmt.Fprintln(w, "partial output...")   // implicitly sends 200 OK right here
	if somethingWentWrong() {
		w.WriteHeader(http.StatusInternalServerError)   // TOO LATE: 200 was already sent
		return
	}
}
```

This compiles and runs without a panic. A `WriteHeader` call after the status is locked in logs a message like `http: superfluous response.WriteHeader call` on the server and has **no effect** on what the client receives, which stays a 200 with whatever partial body was already flushed. Decide the final status code **before** writing any body: buffer the response first if you need to inspect it, or, more commonly, structure the handler so all error checks happen before any success-path writing.

examples.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
)

type greeter struct {
	name string
}

func (g greeter) ServeHTTP(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	fmt.Fprintf(w, "Hello, %s!", g.name)
}

func main() {
	mux := http.NewServeMux()
	mux.Handle("/hello", greeter{name: "Gopher"})
	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", mux)
}

package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
)

func helloHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	fmt.Fprintln(w, "Hello from a plain function!")
}

func main() {
	mux := http.NewServeMux()
	mux.HandleFunc("/hello", helloHandler)   // internally wraps helloHandler in http.HandlerFunc(...)
	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", mux)
}

package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
)

func main() {
	mux := http.NewServeMux()

	mux.HandleFunc("GET /users/{id}", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		id := r.PathValue("id")
		fmt.Fprintf(w, "fetching user %s", id)
	})

	mux.HandleFunc("DELETE /users/{id}", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		id := r.PathValue("id")
		fmt.Fprintf(w, "deleting user %s", id)
	})

	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", mux)
}

