encoding/json offers two related ways to turn JSON text into a Go value: `json.Unmarshal(data []byte, v interface{})`, which needs the entire JSON payload already sitting in memory as a byte slice, and `json.NewDecoder(r io.Reader).Decode(v interface{})`, which reads from any `io.Reader`, streaming through the input rather than needing it all up front. In an HTTP handler `r.Body` is an `io.ReadCloser` (so it is an `io.Reader`, the same small-interface composition from the Packages concept), which makes the decoder the natural fit:

```go
type CreateUserRequest struct {
	Name string `json:"name"`
	Age  int    `json:"age"`
}

func createUserHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	var req CreateUserRequest
	if err := json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(&req); err != nil {
		http.Error(w, "invalid request body", http.StatusBadRequest)
		return
	}
	// req.Name and req.Age are now populated
}
```

Using `json.Unmarshal` here would mean calling `io.ReadAll(r.Body)` to buffer the whole body into a `[]byte` and then unmarshaling it: an extra allocation and an extra pass that `Decode` avoids by parsing as bytes arrive. For a small JSON payload the difference is negligible, but a service handling large or numerous bodies should not buffer each one unbounded, and in either case you should cap the size with `http.MaxBytesReader`. Note the `&req`: `Decode` needs a pointer so it can fill in the value the caller holds, the same value-vs-pointer reasoning as in the Structs and Methods concept. Passing `req` by value would fill a copy the handler never sees (and `Decode` would return an `InvalidUnmarshalError`). `http.Error` sets `Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8`, writes the message plus a newline, and sends the status code, so it is also a header-before-body call and must come before any other write.

Encoding follows the same Reader/Writer symmetry: `json.NewEncoder(w io.Writer).Encode(v interface{})` writes JSON directly to any `io.Writer`, and `http.ResponseWriter` satisfies that interface, so encoding straight to the response is the idiomatic pattern:

```go
func getUserHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	user := User{Name: "Amy"}
	w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")   // MUST happen before Encode's first Write
	if err := json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(user); err != nil {
		log.Printf("failed to encode response: %v", err)
	}
}
```

This applies the ResponseWriter ordering rule from the Handlers and Routing concept: `Encode` writes body bytes, which implicitly sends `200 OK` and the headers on its first internal `Write` if nothing was sent before. Setting `Content-Type` **after** `Encode` is too late, because the headers have already gone out, so the header map mutation has no effect on the response. Without a Content-Type, net/http sniffs one from the body, which for JSON text gives `text/plain; charset=utf-8`. The order is always headers first, then body. This is a common bug in hand-written handlers because it compiles, and it can look fine when tested with tools that ignore Content-Type.

examples.code:
package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"net/http"
)

type CreateUserRequest struct {
	Name string `json:"name"`
	Age  int    `json:"age"`
}

func createUserHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	var req CreateUserRequest
	if err := json.NewDecoder(r.Body).Decode(&req); err != nil {
		http.Error(w, "invalid request body: "+err.Error(), http.StatusBadRequest)
		return
	}

	w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
	w.WriteHeader(http.StatusCreated)
	json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(req)
}

package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
)

type Profile struct {
	Name     string `json:"name"`
	Nickname string `json:"nickname,omitempty"`
	password string `json:"password"`   // unexported -- the tag has NO effect here at all
}

func main() {
	p := Profile{Name: "Amy", Nickname: "", password: "secret123"}
	data, _ := json.Marshal(p)
	fmt.Println(string(data))
	// {"name":"Amy"}
	// Nickname is omitted (it's the zero value, "", and tagged omitempty)
	// password is ALWAYS omitted -- it's unexported, so json can't see it, tag or not
}

